<content:encoded>

<![CDATA[ <div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png" width="1456" height="1456" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/ed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1456,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:267160,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/png&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://thekintsugisamurai.substack.com/i/219280246?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Klta!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fed559311-a32d-4c3f-b88a-49e17ffe7a72_1620x1620.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon-noB79L"><g><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/young-americans-spend-much-more-time-alone-than-they-did-fifteen-years-ago</p><p></p><p>The American Time Use Survey (ATUS) is one of the best time-use datasets in the world. The Bureau of Labor Statistics has run it annually since 2003, drawing a nationally representative sample from Current Population Survey respondents (usually 9,000&#8211;26,000 per year). It uses 24-hour time diaries rather than asking people to recall or estimate &#8212; diary methods consistently outperform recall-based surveys for this kind of question. So the underlying instrument is sound and the trend is real.</p><p>&#8220;Alone&#8221; has a specific meaning. It means no one is physically present &#8212; but a two-hour FaceTime with friends counts as alone. Over 15 years that saw smartphones and video calls explode, this quietly reframes what the number means. &#8220;More time without a body in the room&#8221; isn&#8217;t the same as &#8220;more time socially isolated.&#8221;</p><p>COVID distorts the endpoint. 2020 data excludes March 18&#8211;May 9 (lockdown), and the pandemic broke habits that hadn&#8217;t reset by 2023. A 2010 vs. 2019 comparison (18%, still real but less dramatic) is a cleaner read on the pre-pandemic trend.</p><p>15&#8211;29 is a wide bracket. It mixes 15-year-olds living at home with 29-year-olds who may live alone, work remotely, etc. Shifts in living arrangements (later partnering, more solo living in late 20s) could drive part of this independent of behavior change.</p><p>2010 baseline is post-recession. Young people had unusually high co-residence with family then (couch-surfing with parents during a bad job market), which may make the baseline artificially low on &#8220;alone time.&#8221;</p><p>Sample sizes shrink for subgroups. The 15&#8211;29 slice is smaller than the overall sample, so year-to-year wobble is larger than the headline chart suggests.</p><p>Bottom line: the direction and rough magnitude are there. The &#8220;45%&#8221; figure is a real finding, not a methodological artifact. But it&#8217;s measuring physical solitude, not loneliness, and some of the increase reflects structural shifts (video calls replacing in-person hangouts, delayed partnering) rather than purely a behavioral collapse.</p><p>The population available to the comfortable trap is growing every year. More time without a body in the room, fewer checks against what the mirror shows them. </p><p>These notes are for those who are looking for a push. </p><p><span data-color="#f1c232" style="color: rgb(241, 194, 50);">Kintsugi Samurai</span></p><p></p> ]]>

</content:encoded>

